 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432323 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432323 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432323 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7fd0a920-47da-5738-85dc-fcfde1071d1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432324 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432324 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432324 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432324 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bcc7880c-e1d9-51f3-9d82-55e1eee95989" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432325 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432325 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432325 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432325 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432325 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432325 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432325 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432325 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432325 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ed11c1c1-5852-58e2-a3f1-e49b2bb10b4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432326 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432326 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432326 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432326 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{2}+6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{23}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432326 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a08d141-4335-56a2-8562-7ce4a6f3683b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432326 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{2}+6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-30{q}_{1}+5{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=8{q}_{1}-4{q}_{2}+4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -30 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{23}=3+8+(-4)=7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432327 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432327 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95917" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432327 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432327 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $4\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is a $4\\times 2$ upper triangular matrix\n\nII. The columns of $Q$ are unit vectors\n\nIII. $R$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432327 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b19eecb-3ecc-5584-a617-4bf8675e0b01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432327 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be a $4\\times 2$ matrix, but is not necessarily upper triangular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis, they are unit vectors.\n\n- Statement III is false. $R$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432328 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432328 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432328 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432328 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)$ is spanned by the columns of $Q$\n\nIII. $R$ is a square matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432328 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71004f41-ae2f-50fd-9fdc-88ec92c18337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432328 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be an $m\\times n$ matrix, so $Q$ is not necessarily a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A),$ they must span $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $R$ must be an $m\\times n$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432329 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432329 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41421" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432329 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432329 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{11}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432329 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8825d6b3-93be-5a03-9cc7-5000a40034aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432329 #edb/ref 1074 "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{9}{9})[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{11}=(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (3)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=2\\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432330 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432330 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96015" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432330 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432330 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432330 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "946d71c0-c7c2-59d9-aaa7-5ecb48201289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432330 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432331 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432331 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432331 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432331 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $8\\times 4$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is an $8\\times 4$ matrix\n\nII. $R$ is an $8\\times 8$ matrix\n\nIII. $R$ is an upper triangular matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432331 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6266ee7-76af-56d5-9957-fa402398ddd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432331 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a  QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $Q$ must be an $8\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. $R$ should be a $4\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed $R$ must be an upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432332 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432332 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432332 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432332 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432332 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c68cf512-7931-57e9-a96b-53e13136a3ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432332 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432333 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432333 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432333 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432333 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432333 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1307433-5f5d-5677-8396-c15cc88708bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432333 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432334 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432334 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432334 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432334 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=2,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432334 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5f7e59e-edf4-549f-aea0-27ed8989142e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432334 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{1}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}={v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}={v}_{1}+{v}_{2}={q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=-{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=-{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=1+(-1)+3=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
